From d8de30a83e396b74a33b2dc4560fe9f0182218e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:25:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] fix(#53): SWAP never transposes two identical operands,
 so no mutant equals its original

The defect, verbatim: the SWAP operator ("transpose call arguments") transposes two arguments that are
identical expressions. The mutant it produces is the original, byte for byte.
`def f(x): return g(False, False, x)` gave `SWAP_174b8740 | transpose call arguments | identical to
original: True` (Wesker 2fbd029). Such a mutant can only survive; it enlarged the universe and the
denominator, a report printed it with an empty diff, and Detective's DONE block offered `flag` for it
(it surfaced on `cli.py::candidate_equivalent_caveat`, which calls `residual_disposition(False, False, ...)`).

Why the obvious fix is wrong: recognising the identical mutant after generation (Trivial Compiler
Equivalence already can) leaves it in the universe and the denominator for the length of the run, and
dropping it AFTER the per-call-site budget is spent still lets a repeated operand cost a real question its
place (a call that repeats an operand would ask fewer distinct pairs than the budget allows). So the pair
leaves the candidate list BEFORE the budget: `swap_plan_keyed(keys, budget)` takes the operands' canonical
texts (position-free, so `h(x)` on two lines is the same operand), puts every pair in `distinct` or
`identical`, and spends the budget on `distinct` only.

What each state of the pure decision means: `pair_standing(left_key, right_key)` is `distinct` (the swap
changes the program: a question) or `identical_operands` (the rewrite writes the same text back: not a
mutant, counted by the census as not_applicable and never as asked). `1` and `True`, `1` and `1.0`, `0.0`
and `-0.0` are distinct operands (different canonical text). asked + withheld + identical is always
C(n, 2). `_swap_plan_of(call)` is the one plan generation, the target count and the census read, so they
cannot disagree.

Before: `g(False, False, x)` -> 4 SWAP mutants, one identical to the original. After: 3 (the (0,2) and (1,2)
transpositions and the unwrap), none identical. Policy 9 becomes 10 (the universe lost these mutants; the
corpus gained fp_swap_identical, SWAP 4 -> 3 on it, no existing row moved): one-sign 9.bd305bdfd9f8 ->
10.f7358643c7e0, two-sign 9+neg.f2bb2b1e5f8b -> 10+neg.cad6fd2aabb8.

What was NOT closed: only SWAP's transposition is covered. Other operators whose mutant could equal the
original (a rewrite that writes the same text) are not audited here; the per-site census (#22) reports
what each site did, but this commit adds no cross-operator "mutant equals original" guard beyond SWAP.
Detective's own report of an empty-diff survivor is unchanged by this commit until it regenerates.

The W#55 fixture in tests/test_fresh_arguments_intent.py used the `max(2, 2)` swap as its "mutant identical
to the original"; it no longer exists, so those two tests now assert that NONE is generated (and, were one
ever generated, that none is killed), instead of requiring one.

Receipt: not pinned here (the integrator re-pins `pair_standing` / `swap_plan_keyed` after Wave 4).
Tests: tests/test_swap_identical_operands_intent.py (10) plus test_swap_every_pair_intent.py,
test_swap_plan_intent.py, test_operator_census.py, test_policy.py, test_filter.py, test_engine.py, the
guard files and the #48 files: 413 passed under 3.14.6 and under 3.12.13 (and test_fresh_arguments_intent.py, 60 passed under 3.14.6), PYTHONUNBUFFERED unset. With the
fix disarmed (pair_standing always distinct) 8 of the 10 new tests fail. ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
